ey're still functionally  intact  and they can be integrated into  different things we have created  paper-based sensors based on them we've  created  a bio manufacturing kits that are  freeze-dried  and here we want to apply that to  wearable synthetic biology  and so we have these freeze-dried  cell-free reactions  this could be cell-free this could be a  crispr based reaction we've also tested  various other kinds of synthetic biology  reactions  to make sure they can be freeze-dried  and we can integrate them into different  materials and when these uh freeze-dried  reactions are rehydrated for example by  a contaminated spill you can imagine if  you're wearing  these freeze-dried reactions  and you get a splash on you um it would  rehydrate the freeze-dried reactions  and activate the circuit um and actually  give you uh some kind of signal based on  synthetic biology biosensors of what's  in in your environment so that was our  our primary focus for the past couple of  years in this particular project for  wearable synthetic biology  one of the prototypes we developed  has fluorescent excitation and emission  fiber optics integrated into layers of  the wearable  unit the modular unit here  that in between these two layers you  have freeze-dried cell-free components  that allow for rehydration and sensing  when you're exposed to something and it  also has  portals that allow that liquid to go  inside and rehydrate their reactions  so here's another view of what it looks  like when you  explode the layers  and the freeze-dried reaction  be it a self-free reaction  or a crispr reaction uh occurs it it's  housed right between these emission and  excitation layers  in between the two fiber optics  and here's a different view where you're  looking in between those two layers  on the top you have an emission layer  which collects  any  photons coming from the sensing layer  and it goes to a  digital sensor and you're looking now at  the very right  you're looking now at the bundle uh this  is a cross section of the fiber optic  bundle and so all of those white dots  that's actually  one fiber optic the one fiber from the i  fiber optic bundle that is  being threaded to a particular zone in  the wearable and you can see at the  bottom  um three of those are fluorescent and  that indicates one particular reaction  zone and on the bottom uh we have an  excitation layer  and that is what actually pumps in the  photons to excite whether or not  there is some kind of fluorescent  reporter that has indicated that you  have been exposed to something and so  this is a prototype we developed and i'm  not going to go into the details of  um how it works because that's that's uh  beyond this presentation  but the prototype does work and we  tested various synthetic biology  biosensors in them  including crispr-based sensors  which is what we want to focus on today  and so you look into  the area right between two of the  optical fibers  again excitation on top  uh an emission on the bottom uh or vice  versa  what you can see is uh we can have  um in the reaction freeze-dried here we  have freeze-dried crispr enzymes  and a fluorescent a quenched fluorophore  uh dna probe in there so in this case  this is a cast 12a  and in the presence of a  for example a viral dna or  viral  nucleic acid or bacterial dna for  example  that gets amplified up by  rt-pcr that we put in  we can actually have that uh  that amplicon be detected by cast 12a  and that activates collateral activity  which cleaves the fluorescent reporter  and then you have fluorescence which is  picked up by your emission uh  fiber optic  piece right there on the bottom  and so in that way we can have a  completely freeze-dried system it has  the amplification part  because we've tested the rt rpa and  we've we've shown that that's  stable in a loftwise form uh it has the  detection part which is a cast 12a  we've also tested cast 13 and that also  works and that is also in freeze