 revision phase for this work  um  and the the pandemic kit  and so our lab had to shut down uh and  while we were shut down  we were brainstorming on how we could  uh contribute to  uh pandemic efforts  based on our technology how could we  apply to the technology  it was a trying time um and we were kind  of stuck at home and we were just  basically zooming each other  and we have several physicians  in our laboratory  that were working in covert wards at  that time and we talked to the the  doctors  um and uh what they  mentioned once we presented this to them  was if we could find a way  to do on-site  wearable testing  of copen19  and uh  their their their kind of thought behind  this was  well right now we're in the coveted  wards things are absolutely slammed  and it's taking  over 24 hours just to get uh pcr rtpcr  diagnostics  um to get the results back to these  patients in order to triage them because  you have to know whether or not they  were covered 19 positive in order to  actually put them in the ward um and so  they were like if you could integrate  this with some kind of wearable so where  the doctors don't have to  actually do a lot of the  swabbing and send it off the lab and  then wait for it and just have the  actual  point of care diagnosis happen right  there for the patient that would be  fantastic  and so that's what we worked on um and  so  one thing that we wanted to look at was  is it possible to do  nucleic acid amplification  at the point of care  with minimal instrumentation  and  you know this was around february uh  when the the  the uh  the pandemic was spreading around the  world and in april this is what  uh dr deborah burks the white house  coronavirus response coordinator said  and she quote and i quote there will  never be the ability on a nucleic acid  test to do 300 million tests a day or to  test everyone before they go to work  or school  right and so you know that's very  accurate at that time there really  wasn't any way to do quick nucleic acid  tests  for anyone that wanted it  at home  before you go to work or you know go to  school whenever you wanted on demand  nucleic acid testing was not available  and today it still really isn't  available  and so that's what i'm going to go  on to next  and  so what we developed was we developed a  nucleic acid diagnostic based on crispr  uh detection  for cover 19 that's integrated into a  face mask  so really quickly i'm going to go  through some of the  the key  aspects of our  face mask sensor so it can kind of  ground you into why this technology is  unique and why it's needed  one thing it's sensitive  based on the data that i'm going to show  you we can show that it's comparable  to rt pcr results so the same results  you would that would you would require a  laboratory to perform you can actually  have it completely integrated onto face  mask with our sensor and it's as  sensitive as the who approved rt pcr  assays  also  it's very simplified  as i mentioned there's no laboratory  needed there's actually no instrument  needed there's no electronics there's no  power source  everything is passively driven and  there's there's absolutely no  instrumentation at all and that we think  that's a huge advantage  compared to everything else that is out  there  today  another thing is that it is a complete  point of care solution  so from sample collection it actually  collects a sample  and everything is actually integrated  onto including the analysis so  from  output and result takes only 90 minutes  and that's roughly 45 minutes to 90  minutes that's that's the range of our  our uh the execution of our sensor i  mean the only thing it takes is the  press of a button so that's the only  thing the user requires uh the user  intervention is required is the press of  a button  the sensor is very lightweight you can  hardly notice it it's about one gram  again uh all of the reactions are freeze  dried so it's shelf stable you can  actually store this at room temperature  it's stable  at room temperature um and it the  operation is also completely at room  temperature um therefore you don't  really need any heating to actually make  the reactions uh occur  it's programmable because it's crisper  based you can easily reprogram it to  detect different variants  and  last thing but very important  is that it's inexpensive the add-on cost  of one of these sensors  for a face mask is only five dollars so  that's you know the cup of a fancy  starbucks  latte and and you have a cobia 19  sensor diagnostic that is  completely integrated into your face  mask  and so the idea behind this is you know  if people are already wearing masks in  the middle of a pandemic  we can just  easily add on  a diagnostic to it so while you're  wearing it you're actually collecting  the sample and and it's collecting the  sample from your breath  when you're breathing when you're  talking when you're coughing when you're  sneezing  um and it collects on the sample pad on  the very the inside of the mask  and at the end of the day or or whenever  you uh have collected enough sample you  press the button and it runs the deliver  the analysis to see whether or not you  have kobit 19.  and so this is one of our early early  prototypes um so we're looking at  on the outside what it looks like on the  outside you only have a button and an  indicator  so that the output indicator is a ladder  flow asset strip  on the inside you see all the different  components of our sensor  so our sensor is one unit  it's continuous but it's composed of  four different pieces you have a water  reservoir which contains your water  you have a sample collection zone and  that is a pad that actually collects  breath samples  then you have the  micro pad and that is that houses all  the freeze-dried reactions in a  three-dimensional  paper-based device and then finally that  is connected to lab flow assay which  leads to the outside so that you can see  the result and the lateral flow assay  strip is what gives you the visual  indicator  and so now let's uh  let's run through  what actually happens when you're  wearing the mask and we'll go through  this as uh observed from the inside of  the mask  um  and so if you look on the inside of the  mask the first thing that happens is  sample collection  and we estimate that you'll need about  15 to 20 minutes of wearing this at the  minimum  to collect enough sample  for the detection to occur  and obviously  if you wore it for a longer time  the the results would be much better  because you would be collecting more and  more sample  and so we we do anticipate if this was  to be  produced that we would probably  recommend  wearing the mask for an hour to get  accurate accurate results  so the first thing is sample collection  the patient wears the mask the user  wears the mask  and it clicks on the sample pad which  you can see there  the red dots are indicative of  viral particles for example  and the next step is when you're ready  you activate the sensor and that simply  is the press of a button the button  that's on the outside of the mask in  different versions we actually flip the  the  the water reservoir so it's also on the  outside  but what the pressing the button does is  it connects the water in the reservoir  to the rest of the sensor and so now  that water which was  previously  completely uh  separated from the rest of the system it  actually  connects that and so it allows water to  flow into the system by the press of  that button  and we've also shown that when you do  that you actually do get concentration  uh  of particles in the sample front and i  have some slides to show you that with  some some sample dye for example  and so the sample  gets pushed through the sample pad  and into the micro pad  which is the next layer and that  contains all the different reactions  i'll go throughout that in some more  detail but we have three different  reactions occurring in the micropad a  lysis reaction  an amplification reaction  and then the cast 12a crispr base  detection reaction  when that reaction  is completed  it flows into the ladder flow assay  which gives you a visual readout and the  time between  uh activating the sensor pressing the  button  and getting an actual visual readout we  found it varies between 45 to 90 minutes  and you might think well that's a pretty  large variation there in execution time  and that's because every single sensor  we used  in this study was handmade so everything  was uh you know hand cut  not hand cut but we also we used laser  cutting but everything was assembled by  hand everything was you know  freeze-dried manually and so there was a  lot of error that goes into that  which is something that we're hoping to  to rectify uh in the future  and if we look at  breaking down  that sensor again into its constituent  parts  you have quad reservoir here which is  basically a blister pack containing the  water  sample collection area this is what  collects everything it's a porous uh  substrate that we found that we had  screened for to find  uh something that would  allow lateral flow  and collect sample at the same time  here's a micropad you can see it's kind  of folded in an accordion shape and each  of those spots in the middle are where  the reaction is housed  and then finally your ladder flow assay  output  uh lateral flow output and this was a  commercial lateral flow assay from  millennia biotech that we used  um and here's showing you some of the  the sample flow through the sensor  here on the left we spotted uh just  die blue dye on the sample collection  pad  and then we press the button to allow  the water to flow through the sample pad  and as you can see the  the the dye concentrates at the  rehydration front very similar to what  would happen if you're doing a  paper chromatography for example  um and then it flows directly into the  micropad and that all occurs within you  know six minutes so it's relatively fast  and once it's in the micropad  what you have is you have  three different zones as i mentioned  before a license zone an amplification  which is rtrpa  and then a  cast 12a detection zone  at the end and in between each of these  reactions we have a time delay  the time delay is consist consists of a  molten  and dried  polyvinyl alcohol and these have been  optimized to allow for a time delay of  between 10 to 15 minutes  so the sample sits and hydrates each of  these reactions consecutively for 10 to  15 minutes before moving on to the next  one  and in that way we can actually control  the amount of time  and where the rehydration front is  as it progresses through the sensor  um and to look at some some more detail  into what's going on in the reactions um  so the lysis reaction contains  um detergents that we we combed the  literature we looked at copic 19  uh biophysical papers where they they  had to isolate components of coping 19.  and there's a lot of a wealth of  information on what methods people use  to extract different proteins and and  extract the nucleic acid as well  and we made a list of all of the the  detergent combinations that people have  used  and we screened those to look for ones  that were compatible with our system  that is it has to be freeze dried  and the detergent can't actually  dissolve our micro pad so the micro pad  has  wax layers on it to kind of  prevent everything from flowing outside  of the micropad  and some of the detergents actually  dissolve this wax layer  and so we narrowed it down to slices  components that were efficient  based on literature of lysing the  coronavirus  um  and seemed to work in our system as a  freeze-dried component  then the isothermal amplification this  was actually  a significant part of our  optimization is getting the isothermal  amplification that is the rtrpa  to work at room temperature so all of  this all of these reactions  are hydrated and work at completely  ambient temperatures  and that's something that i really want  to stress it's a huge  advantage of our system compared to  other systems that require heating in  order to work  so after you press that button there's  no other user intervention needed  the flow is driven by capillary action  and everything happens at room  temperature  and the third reaction is a cast 12a  sherlock detection reaction which uses  a fam biotin single-stranded dna probe  so any collateral activity from the cast  12a  as it recognizes  the amplicon from the second  reaction stage  gets translated into a cleavage of that  probe that cleavage is actually detected  in  the lateral flow assay shown on the  right here  so  if you have a negative  covid result  you have two bands and with a strong  positive result you have only one band  show up which is your t-band or your  test band  and what we're actually targeting using  the rt rpa primers  is the spike gene and  the coba gene  in the covid  virus and  we targeted the area of the spike gene  this was back in  2020 of the summer 2020  that we suspected  would most likely  have a lot of mutations this is the the  docking area in the spike gene  um and you know as time has kind of  proven we were actually right so within  our amplicon region here  a lot of these variant mutations uh do  actually occur in that that region  and so uh we could swap out the the grna  for  the  cast 12a to actually target those  different variants and actually give us  a variant  based face mask diagnostic if we wanted  so the diagram shows our primers  uh rt rpa primers as well as in red  where the grna is targeting the amplicon  and at the very end this is what the  lateral flow assay output looks like  it's actually  uh  position oriented so that you have to  bend it backwards  so that it's hidden from public view but  the clinician if if a clinician wants to  see the results all they do is bend the  strip back  or if this is done at home  the the user just has to take the mask  off and bend the strip back once it's  done  all right so  in terms of testing for the sensitivity  these were bench top tests where we  added contrived samples onto the sample  pad  so these were  rna  templates  of covert 19 genomic rna  specifically the spike gene that were  added onto the sample pad  at different numbers copies of target  and what you can see here is that we can  detect it down to  uh  you know 500 copies of target and on the  the  right hand side  you see these are those are  representative ladder flow asset output  uh images that we're showing here and so  down to two orders of magnitude copies  of target  and again each of these the data points  shown in these graphs  is a completely  separate  uh face mask sensor that was fabricated  and assembled  and for hand assembling a lot of these  we think the  the variability in the results is  actually quite good  um and so based on this level of  sensitivity  it's roughly in line with the who  approved rtpcr assays and what you can  expect for the sensitivity of those  next we wanted to look at specificity so  how specific is it for  uh soros kobe 2.  and no surprise there because this is a  crispr based method  and you know it's very highly sensitive  so we tested this against uh spike jeans  that were  uh taken from different  circulating human coronaviruses  so there are four different uh  circulating hemorrhage coronaviruses  that we tested against  and as you can see for all of these um  they're at a very low detection level  whereas with stars kobe 2 we get a very  great a nice positive result there and  as you can see you can see  on the right hand side again  representatives representative images of  the ladder flow assays  from each of the groups that we tested  so at this point  we did much of the testing in vitro this  was early on in the pandemic  early summer  um and the reviewers came back and you  know they wanted us to do to be a bit  more uh  kind of robust in our analysis and they  asked for  whether or not we could do either on  patients or a aerosolized virus  and those were the two things that we  were asked to do  um so the  during that time we didn't think uh just  due to the logistics of where we were we  were in the middle of the summer spike  doing a  clinical trial getting it approved  through the university was just  uh not within the timeline that the  reviewers gave us  and so we looked for the option of uh  aerosolizing the virus and to do that  it took us uh over a month but we  cobbled together  uh a breathing simulator  that was based on  some very kind  loaner equipment from industry where you  have a spontaneous breathing generator  this generates breathing cadence the  right title volume everything very very  accurately  simulating human breathing  we pump that through a system where we  have an inline aerosol production so we  nebulize  um  mrna  uh  target here and so rna is getting  aerosolized through a nebulizer that  goes through a heat sleeve  and then that gets routed through an  anatomically precise airway mannequin so  that it it  simulates the structure  of  the human respiratory system  um  and  the next slide you can see  here this is our reservoir for the  nebulizer where we're actually pipetting  in our sample so this again this is a  spike gene rna that's being propelled in  here  and the aerosols that are generated  using this nebulizer  the aerosols have roughly the same uh  diameter as  small aerosols generated by uh human  breathing so  you know less than five microns  and  here is the rest of the simulator shown  here  i have to  really thank our sponsors  for  loaning us the equipment the test chest  lung simulator  as well as the two sigma  high fidelity airway simulator here  and so that was our system that we used  to simulate  the  um  breathing and aerosolization of  rna  for testing our mask  and with this  um the sample flows through that path  gets 